<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ibrahim Pasha of Egypt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1789">1789</a>–<a href="/years/1848">1848</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ibrahim Pasha of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> was the eldest son of Muhammad Ali and the chief commander of his armies. He destroyed the Saudi capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diriyah">Diriyah</a> in 1818, fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> in the 1820s, and conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> in 1831–1833, defeating the Ottoman army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a>. In 1848 he briefly ruled Egypt as regent for his ailing father.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ibrahim-pasha-of-egypt.jpg" alt="Ibrahim Pasha of Egypt" width="320" height="430" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Charles-Philippe Larivière · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ibrahim_Pasha,_Larivi%C3%A8re.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ibrahim was the first-born son of Muhammad Ali and Amina Hanim, the widow of an Ottoman official. He was born in the village of Nusratli, near Drama in the Ottoman province of Rumelia, and was of Albanian origin through his father. In 1805, at sixteen, he was sent as a hostage to the Ottoman Kapudan Pasha during his father's struggle to establish himself in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and he returned once his father was recognised as Wāli. From 1811 to 1816 he was Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a>, with his seat at Girga, and in 1816 he succeeded his brother Tusun Pasha in command of the Egyptian forces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>His campaign in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> lasted two years and ended in the destruction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-saud">House of Saud</a> as a political power. He followed the Saudis into the desert of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najd">Nejd</a>, and by the end of September 1818 he had forced their leader Abdullah bin Saud to surrender and had taken and sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diriyah">Diriyah</a>. He entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> in triumph on 11 December 1819. In 1824 he sailed for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Morea</a> with 17,000 men to help Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-ii">Mahmud II</a> against the Greek Revolution, landing at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/methoni">Modon</a> on 26 February 1825.</p><p>He brought the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/missolonghi">Missolonghi</a> to an end on 24 April 1826, but was defeated three times in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mani">Mani</a>. In reprisal against Greek guerrillas he devastated the country and sent thousands of inhabitants into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, which aroused indignation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-navarino">Battle of Navarino</a> on 20 October 1827 he evacuated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Morea</a> under the capitulation of 1 October 1828.</p><p>In 1831 he was sent to conquer Ottoman Syria. He took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> on 27 May 1832, defeated Ottoman armies at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Homs</a> and Beilan, and routed the Grand Vizier Reşid Mehmed Pasha at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a> on 21 December. He stayed on as governor, but intensified taxation, conscription, disarmament and forced labour provoked revolts from 1834. In that year he besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-karak">Al-Karak</a> for 17 days and destroyed the town. After his last victory at Nezib on 24 June 1839, the United Kingdom and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> intervened, and he was compelled to evacuate Syria in February 1841.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ibrahim spent his last years in peace, but his health was ruined. He visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a> in 1846, and when his father became senile he was appointed regent, holding the office from July until his death on 10 November 1848. In 1872 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isma-il-pasha">Khedive Ismail</a> commissioned a large statue of him by Charles Cordier, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> objected to two reliefs showing their defeats, so it was first erected without them. On the centenary of his death in 1948 the reliefs were rebuilt, and the complete statue stands in a square in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ibrahim Pasha of Egypt” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Ibrahim Pasha</b> besieged and destroyed Diriyah in 1818; the last emir, Abdullah ibn Saud, was executed in Istanbul.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-diriyah">Emirate of Diriyah</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kavala, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. Died in Cairo, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.94,24.41&amp;year=1827" aria-label="Kavala on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Ibrahim Pasha of Egypt"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4639 4061h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4639 4061h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.94,24.41&amp;year=1827">What was here in 1827? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
